.\" -8 is the option that runs this engine. The flag is -8 in both procrule and mdxfind: mdxfind's -u loads a userid file, so -8 is the only spelling that can mean the same thing in both tools. Every rendered occurrence now names it, including the NAME line, the byte-versus-engine comparison tables where -u was the column label, and the three worked examples, which previously printed "procrule: invalid option -- u" if typed as shown. TH 2026-09-15 and procrule 1.25 to 1.34. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -T lint.
.\" rules32(7) -- UTF-32 rule semantics for procrule -u
.\" $Revision: 1.6 $
.\" $Log: rules32.7,v $
.\" Revision 1.6  2026/09/15 12:32:21  dlr
.\" -8 is the option that runs this engine, and the -L example could not demonstrate what it claimed.
.\"
.\" THE FLAG. Every rendered occurrence now names -8, including the NAME line, the byte-versus-engine comparison tables where -u was the column label, and the three worked examples, which printed "procrule: invalid option -- u" if typed as shown. procrule has no -u: its optstring is "?hvVx8qZAL:r:R:t:p:M:m:o:l:s:B:G:D:N:S:H:C:". The flag is -8 in both procrule and mdxfind because mdxfind -u loads a userid file, so -8 is the only spelling that can mean the same thing in both tools.
.\"
.\" THE LOCALE EXAMPLE, which was wrong and whose stated reason was also wrong. It showed l on ISTANBUL yielding istanbul and dotless-i stanbul, with -L tr selecting the latter and -L c the former. Measured: that word returns istanbul under every locale, including -L tr. The page explained the ASCII case as "a word that never reaches an ambiguous mapping produces one candidate", which is not what happens -- capital I IS the ambiguous mapping. The real reason is dispatch: under -8 each word is routed by its own content, and a word that is entirely ASCII is run by the BYTE engine, which has no locale, so neither -L nor any other UTF-32 semantic can change its result. -L was correct throughout; the example was simply built on a word that never reaches the engine.
.\"
.\" Both are fixed. The explanation now states the dispatch rule and says plainly that -8 looks like it does nothing on a pure-ASCII wordlist and that this is correct rather than broken. The example uses ISTANBUL-E-ACUTE, and carries a line noting that plain ISTANBUL gives one result under every locale. Verified: the non-ASCII word gives both variants by default, dotless i under -L tr, plain i under -L c, which is what the page now shows.
.\"
.\" TH 2026-09-15, procrule 1.25 to 1.34. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -T lint.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.5  2026/09/11 15:34:42  dlr
.\" The class limitation under -u is no longer a hazard, so rewrite it as what it now is: a loud refusal. ruleproc32.c gained a read_class_operand() wrapper at the eight class-capable operand slots, so every class form in both syntaxes is refused at load time naming the rule, and an explicit case for the tilde means the diagnostic names the prefix rather than whichever verb follows it. The section previously tabulated which half of the family failed silently and which loudly, because seven of the nine John-form verbs took the question mark as a literal operand and re-read the class letter as the next verb: @?d was purge-the-digits over bytes and purge-a-literal-question-mark-then-duplicate under -u, @?l was P1 against pass1 x, and four more silently rejected everything. None of that is reachable now. A class rule either fails here or is absent from the run; it cannot mean one thing over bytes and another under -u, and that is the sentence the page needed. The escape is the part that changed in the users favour and it was missing from the old text entirely: the doubled form now means the same in both engines, so a rule file corrected from @? to @?? runs identically either way, which is what makes the migration safe for anyone using -u. Verified across every unprefixed form. The parity is stated as holding for the unprefixed forms only, because a tilde rule is refused under -u even when its operand is the escape, so ~@?? works over bytes and fails here. Also recorded that a question mark which is an ordinary operand to a verb taking no class is untouched, since $? ^? i0? and o1? are identical in both engines and a reader has no way to know that without being told. The heading changes from and are not uniformly refused to and are refused. Classes under -u remain unimplemented and that is still a later pass, so the section stays in LIMITATIONS and stays deletable whole. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -T lint.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.4  2026/09/11 15:22:05  dlr
.\" Record the character-class hazard under -u, which is a silent-wrong-answer case rather than an ordinary missing feature. The byte engine now takes a class operand on nine verbs in two syntaxes and the UTF-32 compiler has no class notation at all, but it does not refuse the whole family: the ~ forms, s?CY and ?? itself are refused loudly, while the other seven John-form verbs take the ? as the literal operand and re-read the class letter as the next verb. The rule compiles, runs, and means something else. @?d is purge-the-digits over bytes and purge-a-literal-? then duplicate under -u, so Pass1 x becomes Pass x one way and Pass1 xPass1 x the other; @?l is P1 against pass1 x. The reject verbs fail in the opposite direction, where =0?u $Z keeps the word over bytes and rejects it under -u because =0? asks whether position 0 holds a question mark, and neither case prints anything. The split between loud and silent is tabulated so a reader can tell at a glance which half a rule falls in, and the section says plainly not to combine a class rule with -u. Written so it can be deleted whole if the UTF-32 engine gains classes, in the same shape as the dot-verb and backslash sections removed at 1.3. The S entry is again left untouched pending the shift-case restructure. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -T lint.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.3  2026/09/11 14:08:37  dlr
.\" Two limitations documented here no longer exist, and one new verb needs a refusal recorded. The dot-verb section said .N at the last position reads stale buffer and told the reader to treat it as unspecified under -u. Both engines now no-op there, matching hashcat, as do all six GPU kernels, so the section is removed and . and , are recorded among the verbs that do not change: both are no-ops where the neighbour does not exist, .N at the last position and ,0 at the first, and a rule using .N at the final position is now specified rather than order-dependent. The backslash section said the UTF-32 engine has no escape processing at all and that the failure is quiet, with ^\ex41 prepending a backslash and then re-reading x41 as the extract verb. That was closed in ruleproc32.c 1.10: escapes are implemented for the $ and ^ operands, which are the only two verbs the byte engine runs PARSEHEX over, and below 0x80 the engines now agree exactly. The section is rewritten around the residual divergence, which is real and worth keeping: at 0x80 and above the byte engine inserts the raw byte while the UTF-32 engine refuses the rule loudly, because a byte is not a codepoint and reading \exC3 as U+00C3 would invent a reading the engine makes nowhere else. New subsection for B, the add-byte-value verb: it is byte arithmetic by definition, hashcat wraps it at 256, and there is no codepoint reading that is not an invention, so it is refused under -u exactly as control-B is. Noted also that B is hashcat-master-only, absent from the 6.2.5 release and from John. The standalone colon now emits nothing in both engines, per ruleproc32.c 1.11, and that is stated with the other no-ops. The S entry is left untouched: the verb is being restructured to John paired keyboard table and will be rewritten with that change. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -T lint.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.2  2026/09/06 14:53:54  dlr
.\" The literal-quote divergence is gone: describe the two-pass parse.
.\"
.\" A rule may use a double quote as an operand, which reads to the parser as a quoted operand that never closes. The compiler now asks the parser twice -- quoted operands first, then quotes as plain characters -- rather than guessing from quote counts, since the line is still available. Rules that compile under the first reading keep it, and rules that compile under neither are still refused. Of the 17,320 discovery catalog rules the 138 containing a literal quote were refused here and accepted by the byte engine; the two now agree exactly.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/09/05 01:36:31  dlr
.\" Initial revision: rules32(7). Documents the UTF-32 rule engine selected by procrule -u -- codepoint operands and quoted strings, 32-bit arithmetic and shift verbs, grapheme-cluster positions and lengths and the full list of what does and does not join into a cluster, the case layer including the growing uppercases and Greek final sigma, -L and the locale-ambiguous dotted I with the measured cost of emitting both, the verbs that do not change, and the traps: normalisation, invalid UTF-8 dying silently, control-B and backslash escapes unsupported, and the dot verb reading stale buffer at the last position. Every example was run against ruleproc32.c 1.7 before it was written down.
.\"
.TH RULES32 7 "2026-09-15" "procrule 1.34" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
rules32 \- UTF\-32 rule semantics under \fBprocrule \-8\fR
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B procrule
.B \-8
.RB [ \-L
.IR locale ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR rulefile ]...
.I wordlist
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR procrule (1)
normally interprets a mangling rule over
.IR bytes .
The
.B \-8
option selects a second engine that runs the whole pipeline in UTF\-32:
each input line is decoded from UTF\-8 to codepoints, the rule is
interpreted against codepoints, and the result is encoded back to UTF\-8.
.PP
The byte engine is untouched and remains the default.  Rule syntax is the
same in both, and a rule file needs no changes.  This page documents only
where the two deliberately differ.
.PP
Under
.B \-8
the input must be well\-formed UTF\-8.  A line that is not is dropped; see
.SM LIMITATIONS
below.
.PP
.B \-L
implies
.BR \-8 .
.PP
Non\-ASCII characters are written on this page as
.BR <U+NNNN> ,
because a man page cannot carry them.  In a rule file and a wordlist they
are written literally, as UTF\-8.
.SH "OPERANDS ARE CODEPOINTS"
The byte engine packs each rule operand into a single byte, so a rule whose
operand is a multi\-byte character cannot be compiled there at all: it is
reported as
.B "Invalid rule line. Ignored."
and dropped before the engine ever sees it.  Under
.B \-8
an operand is a codepoint, and the same rule works.
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule   $<U+1F633>          append one emoji
word   agrumato
\-8     agrumato<U+1F633>
bytes  rule rejected
.fi
.RE
.PP
This applies to every verb that takes a literal operand:
.BR $ ,
.BR ^ ,
.BR s ,
.BR @ ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR ! ,
.BR / ,
.BR ( ,
.BR ) ,
.BR 3 ,
.B e
and
.BR v .
.SS "Quoted operands"
An operand may instead be a quoted string, which the byte engine has no
spelling for at all.  A doubled quote inside the quotes is one literal
quote.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$"123"        password    ->  password123
s"abc" "XY"   abcabc      ->  XYXY
s"b" "BB"     abcabc      ->  aBBcaBBc
@"bc"         abcabc      ->  aa
i2"XY"        password    ->  paXYssword
o0"AB"        password    ->  ABassword
v2"--"        abcdefgh    ->  ab--cd--ef--gh--
31"--"        foo--bar--baz  ->  foo--bar--Baz
e"--"         foo--bar--baz  ->  Foo--Bar--Baz
$""""         password    ->  password"
.fi
.RE
.PP
A quoted operand can be something with no single\-codepoint spelling: a
Devanagari conjunct, a vowel carrying two marks, a joiner sequence.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$"<U+0915><U+094D><U+0937>"   x    ->  x followed by that conjunct
$"<U+1F469><U+200D><U+1F4BB>" dev  ->  dev followed by that sequence
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B s
is the only verb taking two operands.  A space or tab
.I between
two quoted operands is skipped, so
.B "s\(dqab\(dq \(dqcd\(dq"
reads as two operands where
.B "s\(dqab\(dq\(dqcd\(dq"
would read as the single operand
.BR ab\(dqcd .
A separator is
.I not
skipped after an unquoted operand, because a space is a legitimate
operand:
.B "s_ "
substitutes an underscore for a space, and four rules in the first thousand
of a real rule file do exactly that.
.PP
A quote is also an ordinary character to append, insert or substitute, so
a rule may use one as an operand:
.B $\(dq
appends a double quote.  That
reads, to the operand parser, as a quoted operand that opens and never
closes.  Rather than guess from the number of quotes on the line, the
compiler asks the parser twice: once with quoted operands enabled and, only
if that fails, once with quotes as plain characters.  The line is still
available at that point, so nothing is lost by trying again.
.PP
A rule that compiles under the first reading keeps it, so quoted operands
are unaffected.  A rule that compiles under neither is still refused: an
unterminated quote followed by text that is not valid rule syntax, such as
.BR $\(dqabc ,
fails both passes and is reported.  This removes what was
previously the only divergence from the byte engine \(em of the 17,320
rules in the discovery catalog, the 138 containing a literal quote were
refused here and accepted there; the two now agree exactly.
.SS "Prepending a string is not a chain of prepends"
Each
.B ^
pushes in front of the last, so
.B ^a^b^c
yields
.BR cbaword .
A quoted prepend is written as a string and lands as one.
.PP
.RS
.nf
^a^b^c     word  ->  cbaword
^"abc"     word  ->  abcword
.fi
.RE
.SS "Append and prepend runs"
A run of adjacent appends or prepends compiles to one operation, as it
does in the byte engine.  The output is identical; the property is that a
chain of twenty\-four year and suffix appends costs what one costs, rather
than growing with the length of the chain.
.SH "ARITHMETIC AND SHIFTS ARE 32-BIT"
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.B L
and
.B R
operate on the whole codepoint rather than on a byte.  This is the one
change that can alter the result of an ASCII rule.
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule   L0            shift position 0 left one bit
word   A            (U+0041)
\-8     U+0082       encoded as the two bytes C2 82
bytes  the single byte 0x82
.fi
.RE
.PP
On ASCII the two agree wherever the value stays in range.  Divergence is
rare but real: applying the first thousand rules of a production rule file
to six ASCII words produced 5,867 candidates, of which exactly three
differed, all from one rule.
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule   $E L7
word   4aD4aD4a
\-8     4aD4aD4<U+00C2>E
bytes  4aD4aD4 <0x82 raw> E
.fi
.RE
.PP
These four verbs are also the only way to produce a value UTF\-8 cannot
represent.  A codepoint pushed past U+10FFFF or into the surrogate block
D800\-DFFF is
.I dropped
from the output and the rest of the line is emitted.
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule   L0
word   <U+6C00>ab      U+6C00 << 1 is U+D800, a surrogate
\-8     ab
.fi
.RE
.SH "POSITIONS AND LENGTHS COUNT GRAPHEME CLUSTERS"
This is the largest change.  Every position and length operand counts
.I grapheme clusters
\(em what a reader counts as characters \(em not codepoints.  A rule that
says delete character three deletes the third thing a reader sees, and on
vocalised Arabic or pointed Hebrew the third codepoint is routinely a vowel
mark hanging off the second letter.
.PP
Affected verbs:
.BR T ,
.BR D ,
.BR ' ,
.BR z ,
.BR Z ,
.BR y ,
.BR Y ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR x ,
.BR O ,
.BR * ,
.BR v ,
.BR _ ,
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
and the verbs that reorder or remove without an index:
.BR r ,
.BR f ,
.BR q ,
.BR k ,
.BR K ,
.BR { ,
.BR } ,
.B [
and
.BR ] .
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule   ]                         drop the last character
word   <U+0939><U+093F><U+0928><U+094D><U+0926><U+0940>   Hindi "hindii"
\-8     <U+0939><U+093F>          drops the whole final conjunct
bytes  drops one byte, leaving invalid UTF-8
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B _
is the exception worth knowing: it compares against the length of the
.I original
input, in clusters, not against the running length.
.B <
and
.B >
compare against the current buffer.
.SS "What forms one cluster"
.TP
Combining marks
Categories Mn, Mc and Me \(em 2,295 codepoints in 290 ranges.  This covers
Arabic harakat, Hebrew niqqud, Thai vowel signs, NFD Latin, and a Yoruba
vowel carrying a dot below and a tone mark at once.
.TP
Zero\-width joiner sequences
U+200D and what follows it, which is what makes an emoji profession or a
family one unit rather than two or three people who come apart under a
reverse.
.TP
Zero\-width non\-joiner
U+200C extends the cluster it follows.  In Devanagari it is written
deliberately between two consonants to suppress a conjunct; left to start a
cluster of its own it produced a word beginning with a bare non\-joiner.
.TP
Regional indicator pairs
U+1F1E6 to U+1F1FF taken two at a time, which is how a flag is encoded.
Reversing by codepoint turned the US flag into SU \(em a different flag,
not a mangled one.
.TP
Emoji modifiers
The Fitzpatrick skin tones U+1F3FB to U+1F3FF, category Sk, which the
combining\-mark test does not catch.
.TP
Variation selectors and enclosing keycaps
Caught by the combining\-mark test.  A keycap such as digit, U+FE0F,
U+20E3 is one cluster.
.TP
Hangul conjoining jamo
UAX #29 rules GB6 to GB8.  A Korean syllable can be stored precomposed or
as separate leading consonant, vowel and trailing consonant; macOS stores
filenames decomposed, so harvested Korean arrives either way.
.TP
Indic conjuncts
UAX #29 rule GB9c, for the six scripts Unicode defines
Indic_Conjunct_Break for: Devanagari, Bengali, Gujarati, Oriya, Telugu and
Malayalam.  Consonant, virama, consonant is one conjunct, so a drop no
longer leaves a dangling halant.
.TP
Khmer coeng
U+17D2, added beyond the Unicode property.  Coeng is defined as combining
with the consonant that
.I follows
to form a subscript, so it cannot end a word; reversing Khmer left one
trailing, which is malformed text.
.TP
Halfwidth katakana sound marks
U+FF9E and U+FF9F.  Unicode files these as modifier
.I letters,
so the combining\-mark test walks straight past them, but UAX #29 puts them
in Extend.  Halfwidth katakana is still everywhere in Japanese banking and
legacy systems, and without this a voiced kana came apart into a bare
letter and a floating mark.
.SS "What is deliberately not joined"
The viramas of Tamil, Kannada, Gurmukhi, Sinhala, Myanmar and Tibetan do
.I not
join.  Unicode excludes them from Indic_Conjunct_Break, and for Tamil that
is plainly right: its pulli is always visible, and a consonant carrying one
is a normal standalone letter, so joining would merge units a reader sees
as separate.  The other five are a judgement call left at the standard's
default rather than decided here.
.SH "CASE MAPPING"
The byte engine cases ASCII only.  The UTF\-32 engine uses a generated
table of 2,688 one\-to\-one mappings over 24 ranges covering 23 cased script
blocks, from Latin\-1 and Greek and Cyrillic through Georgian, Cherokee,
Armenian, Glagolitic, fullwidth Latin, Deseret, Osage, Old Hungarian,
Warang Citi, Medefaidrin and Adlam.
.SS "c and C capitalise the first alphabetic character"
.B c
lowercases the word and then raises the first
.I alphabetic
character, skipping leading digits and punctuation.  This matches the byte
engine and is
.I not
hashcat's
.BR c ,
which raises position 0 whatever it is.
.B C
is the exact mirror.
.PP
.RS
.nf
c    4aD4aD4a  ->  4Ad4ad4a       (hashcat gives 4ad4ad4a)
C    4aD4aD4a  ->  4aD4AD4A
.fi
.RE
.PP
"Alphabetic" here means "has a case mapping", so these verbs reach every
script the table covers rather than just A\-Z.
.SS "Uppercase can grow"
A hundred and three codepoints have a case mapping that changes length and
are therefore excluded from the one\-to\-one table.  A hundred and two of
them have an uppercase longer than one codepoint, and a second table
carries those: 79 Greek
Extended forms, the 12 Latin ligatures, 5 Latin Extended Additional, 2
Greek, 3 in Latin\-1 and Latin Extended\-A including the German eszett, 1
Latin Extended\-B and 1 Armenian.  The widest produces three codepoints.
The hundred and third, U+0130, has a lowercase that grows and is mapped to
plain
.B i
by policy.
.PP
Only the whole\-string verbs
.BR u ,
.B C
and
.B c
expand.  The position\-indexed verbs
.B T
and
.BR 3 ,
and the whole\-string
.BR t ,
.B E
and
.BR e ,
stay one\-to\-one, because growing a character underneath an index would
move every position the rest of the rule refers to.
.PP
.RS
.nf
u    stra<U+00DF>e   ->  STRASSE     six characters become seven
t    stra<U+00DF>e   ->  STRA<U+00DF>E
uD6  stra<U+00DF>e   ->  STRASS      D6 now names a character that
                                     did not exist before u ran
u    <U+FB03>x       ->  FFIX        the ffi ligature, three out
u    <U+1FB4>        ->  <U+0386><U+0399>   Greek Extended
u    <U+FB13>        ->  <U+0544><U+0546>   Armenian, one script to another
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B c
raises the first cased character, which is uppercase and not Unicode
.I title
case.  On the fi ligature that is visible:
.PP
.RS
.nf
c    <U+FB01>nd  ->  FInd        title case would give Find
u    <U+FB01>nd  ->  FIND
.fi
.RE
.SS "Greek final sigma"
The table maps capital sigma to the medial small sigma U+03C3.  A separate
pass run after a whole\-word lowercase rewrites a word\-final one to U+03C2.
End of word means end of buffer, or a following character that is not a
cased letter.
.PP
.RS
.nf
l    <U+039F><U+0394><U+039F><U+03A3>
     ->  <U+03BF><U+03B4><U+03BF><U+03C2>       word-final: U+03C2
l    <U+039F><U+0394><U+039F><U+03A3><U+039C><U+039F><U+03A5>
     ->  <U+03BF><U+03B4><U+03BF><U+03C3><U+03BC><U+03BF><U+03C5>
                                       medial: U+03C3
.fi
.RE
.SH "LOCALE VARIANTS AND -L"
The dotted and dotless I is the only mapping in the covered ranges with
more than one right answer.  Turkish and Azeri uppercase
.B i
to U+0130 and lowercase
.B I
to U+0131; every other locale gives
.B I
and
.BR i .
Nothing in a candidate says which system stored the password.
.PP
By default both variants are emitted, which cannot miss a candidate.  A
locale is a property of the storing system, not of a character, so a
Turkish reading applies to every
.B i
in the word at once and the count stays at two rather than two to the power
of the number of ambiguous characters.  A word that never reaches an
ambiguous mapping produces one candidate.
.PP
An ASCII word produces one candidate for a different and more important
reason: under
.B \-8
each word is routed by its own content, and a word that is entirely ASCII is
run by the BYTE engine, which has no locale.  So
.B \-L
cannot change the result for such a word, and neither can any other UTF\-32
semantics.  This is why the example below uses a word with a non\-ASCII
character in it \(em with a pure\-ASCII word every locale returns the same
single line and
.B \-L
looks broken when it is working exactly as specified.
.PP
.B \-L
pins the locale and only ever
.IR narrows :
a wrong declaration costs coverage rather than producing wrong candidates.
.TP
.B \-L tr
.PD 0
.TP
.B \-L az
.TP
.B \-L turkish
.TP
.B \-L azeri
.PD
The Turkish and Azeri mapping.
.TP
.B \-L c
.PD 0
.TP
.B \-L root
.TP
.B \-L posix
.TP
.B \-L none
.PD
The mapping everyone else uses.
.PP
Values are matched without regard to case.  An unrecognised value is a
usage error and exits 1.  Omitting
.B \-L
keeps the exhaustive default.
.PP
.RS
.nf
l           ISTANBUL<U+00C9>  ->  istanbul<U+00E9>
                           <U+0131>stanbul<U+00E9>
\-L tr  l    ISTANBUL<U+00C9>  ->  <U+0131>stanbul<U+00E9>
\-L c   l    ISTANBUL<U+00C9>  ->  istanbul<U+00E9>

the same rule on plain ISTANBUL gives istanbul under every locale: an
all-ASCII word is run by the byte engine, which has no locale
.fi
.RE
.PP
The default is measurably wasteful.  Fifty thousand words of
.I /usr/share/dict/words
under the four rules
.BR l ,
.BR u ,
.B c
and
.B C
produce 149,992 candidates with
.B \-L
pinned and 207,976 without: 38.7% more output, of which 27.9% is a locale
the operator already knows is wrong.  A Turkish list has the same problem
in reverse.
.SH "VERBS THAT DO NOT CHANGE"
.TP
.BR H ", " h
Hex\-encode the word.  These agree with the byte engine for
.I all
input, not just ASCII: the byte engine hexes raw bytes, procrule's input is
UTF\-8, so hexing the UTF\-8 serialisation of the codepoints reproduces it
exactly.  Hexing the 32\-bit scalars would give eight digits per character
and disagree on every word.
.TP
.B S
An mdxfind extension, not a hashcat verb: replace every
.B a
or
.B A
with a newline.  Deliberately still ASCII\-only, because the verb names a
byte value; widening it to every character that looks like an
.B a
would change which words it fires on.
.TP
.BI . N " and " , N
.BI . N
replaces the character at
.I N
with the one after it and
.BI , N
with the one before it.  Both are no\-ops where the neighbour does not
exist \(em
.B .N
at the last position,
.B ,0
at the first \(em in both engines and in all six GPU kernels, which is
hashcat's answer.  Earlier revisions of both engines read one past the end
here and gave order-dependent output; that is fixed, and a rule using
.B .N
at the final position is now specified.
.TP
.BR : ", space, tab"
No\-ops, as before.  A standalone
.B :
compiles to an empty program in both engines and produces no output.
.TP
.B #
Introduces a comment to the end of the rule line.  A rule truncated by one
emits the buffer as it stands.
.SH "LIMITATIONS"
.SS "Normalisation is not performed, and must not be"
The engine does not normalise, in either direction.  A rule file and a
wordlist in different normal forms match nothing, silently, and that looks
exactly like an honest negative.
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule   s<U+00E9>e            substitute precomposed e-acute for e
word   caf<U+00E9>           NFC        ->  cafe
word   cafe<U+0301>          NFD        ->  nothing at all
.fi
.RE
.PP
This cannot be fixed in the engine and should not be.  The hash was
computed over exact bytes; emitting a recomposed candidate would produce a
different digest and crack nothing.  Generating both forms is a
wordlist\-preparation job \(em do it once, up front, and keep both.
.SS "Invalid UTF-8 input is dropped without a diagnostic"
A line that is not well\-formed UTF\-8 dies, and nothing is printed about
it.  The decoder is strict and rejects overlong forms, surrogates encoded
as three bytes, scalars above U+10FFFF, the lead bytes C0, C1 and F5 to FF,
truncated sequences and stray continuation bytes.
.PP
.RS
.nf
file   caf<0xE9>            Latin-1, not UTF-8
       good
rule   $1
\-8     good1
bytes  caf<0xE9>1
       good1
.fi
.RE
.PP
This is the stated contract, not an oversight: after a rule has duplicated,
reversed or truncated the buffer there is no way to say which codepoints
stood for raw bytes, so a round trip cannot be honoured.  Check an unknown
wordlist with
.BR encforce (1)
before running it through
.BR \-8 .
.SS "Character classes are not implemented, and are refused"
The byte engine takes a character class in place of a literal operand on nine
verbs, in two syntaxes \(em John's inline
.BI ? C
and hashcat's
.BI ~? C\fR.
See
.BR mdxfind (1)
for the semantics.
.PP
None of that is implemented here, and every class form is
.I refused at load time,
naming the rule, rather than compiling into something else:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ printf '@?d\en' > cls.rule
$ procrule \-8 \-r cls.rule words.txt
Invalid rule line. Ignored.: @?d
.fi
.RE
.PP
That covers all nine verbs in both syntaxes, and a
.B ~
prefix is refused whatever follows it, so the diagnostic names the prefix
rather than whichever verb comes after.  A class rule therefore either fails
here or is absent from the run; it cannot mean one thing over bytes and another
under
.BR \-8 .
.PP
.B ??
is the exception, and the one form that matters for migration.  The byte engine
requires a literal question mark to be doubled, and the doubled form now means
the same in both engines, so a rule file corrected from
.B @?
to
.B @??
runs identically either way:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule   @??            word pa?ss
bytes  pass
\-8     pass
.fi
.RE
.PP
The parity holds for the unprefixed forms.  A
.BI ~
rule is refused under
.B \-8
even when its operand is the escape, so
.B ~@??
works over bytes and fails here.
.PP
A
.B ?
that is an ordinary operand to a verb which takes no class is untouched:
.BR $? ,
.BR ^? ,
.B i0?
and
.B o1?
behave identically in both engines.
.PP
Supporting classes under
.B \-8
is a later pass.  Until then the refusal is the contract, and it is a loud one.
.SS "B (add byte value) is not supported"
.BI B N X
adds the byte value of
.I X
to the byte at position
.IR N .
It is a byte-arithmetic verb by definition \(em hashcat's own
.B RULE_OP_MANGLE_CHR_ADD
wraps at 256 \(em and there is no codepoint reading of it that is not an
invention, so it is not implemented under
.BR \-8 .
A rule containing it is refused as an invalid rule line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule   B50
word   hello0
bytes  hello\(ga
\-8     refused
.fi
.RE
.PP
The verb is itself hashcat-master-only; it is absent from the hashcat 6.2.5
release and from John.
.SS "Control-B is not supported"
Byte 0x02 is the byte engine's undocumented base64\-encode\-word rule.  It
is not implemented under
.BR \-8 ,
and a rule containing it is refused as an invalid rule line.  It is absent
from the shared verb table, and it appears zero times in 111,000 rules of a
production rule file.
.SS "A backslash escape above 0x7F is refused, not inserted"
Both engines accept
.BI \e x NN
and octal escapes, in a
.B $
or
.B ^
operand only \(em that is the sole pair of verbs the byte engine runs
.B PARSEHEX
over, so
.B s\e1
means a literal backslash in both.  Below 0x80 the two agree exactly:
.B ^\ex41
prepends
.B A
either way, and
.B $\e\e
appends one backslash either way.
.PP
At 0x80 and above they part company.  The byte engine inserts the raw byte;
the UTF\-32 engine refuses the rule, loudly, as an invalid rule line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule   $\exC3
word   ab
bytes  ab<0xC3>    a raw byte, not valid UTF-8 on output
\-8     refused     "Invalid rule line. Ignored.: $\exC3"
.fi
.RE
.PP
This is deliberate.  A byte is not a codepoint, and reading
.B \exC3
as U+00C3 would invent an interpretation the engine makes nowhere else.
The quoted form
.B $"e\(aqacute"
says what was meant unambiguously and is accepted.  Zero rules in 100,000
of a production file use an escape at all; sixty\-six use a literal
backslash as an operand, and those read the same in both engines.
.SS "The cluster definition is not full UAX #29"
A cluster here is the combining\-mark, joiner, regional\-indicator, emoji
modifier, Hangul jamo, Indic conjunct and halfwidth\-sound\-mark set listed
above.  It is not a complete UAX #29 implementation.  No case in the
regression corpus fails today, but a script that needs a rule outside that
set will not be clustered correctly.
.SS "Output is $HEX[] encoded unless -x is given"
This is not new under
.BR \-8 ,
but it is more visible: every byte at or above 0x80 trips the encoder, so
all non\-ASCII output arrives as
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
unless
.B \-x
is used.  That form is what
.BR mdxfind (1)
and
.BR hashpipe (1)
expect, so leave it on when piping between tools and use
.B \-x
only when reading output by eye.
.SH "PERFORMANCE"
The UTF\-32 engine costs roughly four times the byte engine on short ASCII
input.  Measured with
.B "procrule \-B 200000 \-t 1"
on the word
.B password
on an Intel Core i9\-9900K:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rule                    bytes      \-8
$1                      10.1 ns    40.5 ns
u                       13.1 ns    48.8 ns
r                       12.9 ns    65.3 ns
$1$2$3$4$5$6$7$8        11.6 ns    52.1 ns
.fi
.RE
.PP
Those figures are end to end and include the UTF\-8 decode and re\-encode
of every candidate.  The eight\-append rule costs what the one\-append rule
costs in both engines, which is the collapsed\-run property described
above.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Expand a Cyrillic wordlist, keeping
.B $HEX[]
output for
.BR mdxfind (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
procrule \-8 \-r best64.rule russian.txt > candidates.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same, pinned to the non\-Turkish case mapping, for reading by eye:
.PP
.RS
.nf
procrule \-L c \-x \-r case.rule words.txt | less
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check what a rule set actually does to one word before committing a run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
printf 'stra\e303\e237e\en' > /tmp/w    # "strasse" with the eszett
procrule \-8 \-x \-r r.rule /tmp/w
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR procrule (1),
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR hashpipe (1),
.BR encforce (1),
.BR rling (1)
